{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "F10TrueOpPanicsAtEval",
    "description": "REPRO, EXPECTED TO FAIL. The `true` op is a member of the CLOSED evaluable-core operator set: esm-schema.json's `op` description enumerates it explicitly (\"`ifelse`, the calculus form-ops `D` and `ic` ..., `Pre`, `true`, and the array/query ops\"), and esm-spec §4.2 says every binding's evaluator implements each member of that set directly. Under the `bool_and_or` semiring it is the natural body of a SEMI-JOIN -- `exists a matching row` -- which is the shape a most-specific-match key needs everywhere in the MOVES port (the SCC fallback ladders, state-default precedence, the getind.f year rule).\n\nWhat happens instead is a PANIC, not a diagnostic:\n\n    thread 'main' panicked at src/simulate_array/eval.rs:340:18:\n    internal error: entered unreachable code: operator 'true' reached eval_op\n    without an evaluation rule; every entry point must gate with\n    check_evaluable() first\n\nThe message is addressed to the implementor, not the author: it says an entry point failed to gate, which means `check_evaluable()` does not list `true` even though the schema does. `esm validate` passes, so the document is well-formed by the format's own account and dies at evaluation.\n\nWORKAROUND IN USE, and it is why this is only an inconvenience: the same semi-join spelled with a numeric body, `\"semiring\": \"bool_and_or\"` with `\"expr\": 1.0`, evaluates correctly and yields 1 on a match and 0 on none. components/geographic_allocation.esm and the stages after it use that form. If this repro goes green the numeric body can become `true` and read as what it is.",
    "authors": ["EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"],
    "tags": ["finding", "expected-to-fail", "evaluator", "core-ops"]
  },

  "index_sets": {
    "left_rows": { "kind": "interval", "size": 3 },
    "right_rows": { "kind": "interval", "size": 4 }
  },

  "models": {
    "F10TrueOpPanicsAtEval": {
      "tolerance": { "rel": 1e-12 },
      "variables": {
        "left_key": { "type": "unknown", "shape": ["left_rows"], "description": "The probe relation's key column." },
        "right_key": { "type": "unknown", "shape": ["right_rows"], "description": "The target relation's key column: 10 twice, 30 once, 40 once." },
        "keyIsPresent": { "type": "unknown", "shape": ["left_rows"], "units": "1", "description": "Semi-join: does this key appear in the target relation at all? `true` under bool_and_or is the body that says so without pretending to count." }
      },
      "equations": [
        { "lhs": "left_key", "rhs": { "op": "const", "args": [], "value": [10, 20, 30] } },
        { "lhs": "right_key", "rhs": { "op": "const", "args": [], "value": [10, 10, 30, 40] } },
        {
          "lhs": "keyIsPresent",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate", "args": [], "semiring": "bool_and_or", "output_idx": ["l"],
            "ranges": { "l": { "from": "left_rows" }, "r": { "from": "right_rows" } },
            "join": [ { "on": [["left_key", "right_key"]] } ],
            "expr": { "op": "true", "args": [] }
          }
        }
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "a_semi_join_body_may_be_true",
          "description": "INTENDED behaviour, asserted so that this file goes green the day the evaluator gates `true`. Key 10 matches twice and key 30 once -- under the (OR, AND) semiring both read 1, which is the difference from a count. Key 20 matches nothing and reads the OR identity, 0.",
          "time_span": { "start": 0.0, "end": 0.0 },
          "assertions": [
            { "variable": "keyIsPresent", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1.0, "coords": { "left_rows": 1 } },
            { "variable": "keyIsPresent", "time": 0.0, "expected": 0.0, "coords": { "left_rows": 2 } },
            { "variable": "keyIsPresent", "time": 0.0, "expected": 1.0, "coords": { "left_rows": 3 } }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
